    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "L4 solubility and dissolution records are for reference molecules, not the query itself.",
    "Human lopinavir L1 records are qualitative and numeric F values come from rat or formulation studies.",
    "L4 records do not quantify first-pass metabolism or gut-wall efflux for the query.",
    "Structural differences from lopinavir such as thiazole, urea, and carbamate groups may alter metabolism and permeability."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low is strengthened. L4 records for the closest analog lopinavir show crystalline solubility near 3 µg/mL and bulk dissolution of only 12% in 2 hours, which affirmatively supports a poor absorption component. Combined with L1 low-F records for lopinavir and L2 numeric rat F of 5%, the threshold-relevant evidence coherently supports F below 20%. The query's high logP, high molecular weight, high TPSA, and neutral charge are consistent with this poor solubility profile. No L4 record contradicts the low prediction."
}

